# SECURITY — DOUBLE FREE from SAFE, SPEC-DOCUMENTED syntax. Both backends.
#
# `for x in &coll` is the documented way to mutate elements in place
# (docs/language-reference.md:1374, and §9.6's subscript/borrow table names it
# as the spelling that writes through). Assigning to the loop binding over a
# `Vector[String]` double-frees the element storage:
#
#     gg check  ->  OK: no semantic errors            (rc 0, no diagnostic)
#     gg run    ->  free(): double free detected in tcache 2
#                   terminated by SIGABRT (signal 6)
#
# Measured 2026-08-17 on the C backend and reproduced on LLVM. The element type
# matters: a heap-owning element (String) double-frees; a scalar element does
# not, so this samples the resource cell deliberately.
#
# WHY THIS IS SEVERE, beyond the crash: the program is accepted by the safety
# pass with no diagnostic at all, so a memory-safety violation reaches runtime
# from ordinary safe code that the language reference teaches. It is not a
# misuse of an escape hatch — there is no `unsafe` here and no ownership
# operator.
#
# ⚠ Related but DISTINCT from the filed `&`-write-through gap (§9.1's status
# note: the compiler drops the write for a by-value element type). That one
# SILENTLY LOSES a write; this one CORRUPTS THE HEAP.
#
# INTENDED: either the assignment writes through (the specified behaviour) or
# the program is REJECTED at check time with a teaching diagnostic. Crashing is
# neither.
void main():
    Vector[String] d = ["aa", "bb"]
    for s in &d:
        s = "zz"
    print(d.get(0).unwrap())
    print(d.get(1).unwrap())
